10.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The discussion is converging on a coat motif that is both lexical and symbolic. Most colleagues report a canonical pattern of four explicit coat / шинель / greatcoat / overcoat mentions in the Garnett / standard English text, which map onto three substantive symbolic shifts of Otchumyelov/Ochumelov’s chameleon-like fear.

The recurrent beats cited are:
* Entrance / first presentation in the official frock / overcoat / short grey overcoat with muffler, sign of self-importance;
* First fear / deference shift when he buttons / pulls the coat up to his throat, fidgets with buttons/pockets, worries about soiling it, or is nervous about the crowd / possible General;
* Second reversal when he unbuttons / opens / loosens the coat and adopts a harsh / jocular tone toward Khryukin;
* Final reversal / exit when he again hastily buttons / wraps himself in the coat and becomes deferential, hurrying away.

Colleagues 1,10,11,12,15,16,17,18,4,6,7 distinguish four lexical mentions / episodes with three distinct descriptive shifts, often rendered as open→closed→open→closed, with the entrance and final exit sometimes collapsed into three changes of mood. Colleagues 13,14,2,5,8,9 argue for three descriptive uses only, treating the initial entrance and the exit as narrative continuity rather than new descriptive symbolic moments.

Minor divergence on raw counts: Colleague 3 and 6/7 note five nominal mentions of the garment in some versions, with four descriptive reprises. The disagreement is therefore not about the existence of the motif but about counting method: number of named mentions vs number of symbolic re-descriptions.

Current best synthesis: in the received translation the coat is named ~4 times and is symbolically re-described on three substantive occasions, the three shifts being the ones normally quoted as the coat-barometer of fear. The entrance/exit bookends are often counted as a fourth lexical mention. Translation/version differences persist and the precise total depends on whether the initial presentation and the final reprise are counted as descriptive shifts.

12.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The discussion is split between a lexical count of mentions and a symbolic count of descriptive shifts for Ochumelov/Otchumyelov’s шинель/overcoat/frock-coat.

Most colleagues converge on a four-mention / three-shift pattern in the canonical Garnett / usual English text and in the Russian original:
* entrance presentation in the new / short grey / frock / overcoat as the official self-important appearance;
* first handling / buttoning / pulling the coat to the throat / fear of soiling when the dog may be General Zhigalov’s and he becomes servile;
* second reversal: coat opened / unbuttoned / loosened when the dog is pronounced a stray and he can be harsh/jocular;
* final reversal / exit: coat wrapped / re-buttoned / hurried departure when the dog is again thought to be the General’s.

Colleagues 1,10,12,15,16,17,18 describe this as four explicit coat notices/lexical mentions mapping onto three substantive symbolic shifts open → closed → open → closed, i.e. three distinct changes of mood that the coat registers, with the initial presentation and final exit sometimes collapsed. Colleague 12,17,18 explicitly state four explicit mentions, three distinct descriptive/symbolic shifts.

A competing cluster – colleagues 2,5,8,9,13,14 – treats the initial entrance as narrative continuity and counts only three distinct descriptive beats: arrival with coat signalling self-importance, mid-scene fidgeting/buttoning as anxiety about status/contamination, and final exit/humiliated retreat. They state “3 times” descriptive.

Disagreements also involve translation variance: frock-coat/sheepskin/overcoat/greatcoat/shinel’, and whether the first presentation counts as a descriptive symbolic moment or merely a stage direction. Some note five mentions in Russian/English, four descriptive reprises.

I am keeping both numbers in mind: four verbal mentions in the received text, three symbolic descriptive shifts of fear/deference that criticism normally quotes. The precise answer depends on whether the question wants lexical mentions or symbolic descriptive moments. Unresolved is whether the entrance presentation is counted as a “description of the coat” on the same footing as the later handling descriptions.

