1.text:
The coat is a barometer, not a prop. It is brought back into the narrative every time Ochumelov/Otchumyelov changes colour, and the Garnett/Hingley / canonical Russian text does it as a four- mention / three-shift pattern.

Lexically the overcoat / frock-coat / шинель / пальто is named four times in the received editions:

* the entrance presentation – he comes on the scene in his official outer coat, the new / shabby / short grey overcoat with the big white muffler, the outward sign of self-importance;
* the first handling / protection – as soon as the dog may be General Zhigalov’s he pulls/buttons the coat up to his throat, keeps his hands in its pockets and fidgets with it, the coat becoming the measure of his personal risk;
* the second handling / display – when the dog is pronounced a stray he opens / loosens the coat again and can afford a harsh, jocular tone;
* the final wrapping / hurried exit – on the last reversal, when the dog is again thought to be the General’s, he hastily buttons himself in the coat again and becomes deferential again.

Those four lexical re-presentations are normally collapsed in criticism into three substantive symbolic descriptions of the garment as a register of his fearful, chameleon-like mentality:

1. proud official arrival in the coat;
2. anxious protection / fidgeting with the coat;
3. servile retreat with the coat re-buttoned / readjusted on exit.

It is that three-stage open → closed → open → closed mood map, realized in four explicit textual mentions, that the scholarship quotes as the coat = mentality motif. If the initial presentation is not counted as a “shift” the symbolic re-descriptions are three in number, the four mentions being entrance + three shifts.

3.text:
In the 1884 Russian text and in the Garnett/Hingley English tradition the шинель / overcoat / frock-coat is not a background detail, it is the external index of Ochumelov/Otchumyelov’s chameleon-like fear / status anxiety and it is brought back in at every change of colour.

That is one initial presentation plus three reprises:

* Arrival / presentation: Ochumelov is introduced on the scene in his official overcoat / frock-coat, the new / neat garment that is the outward sign of his self-importance and desire to be seen as an official;
* First fear-shift: as soon as the dog’s ownership becomes politically risky he handles / tightens / protects the coat, “to not soil the coat”, the first measure of his anxious self-consciousness;
* Second fear-shift / exit: when the dog is pronounced a stray and again when it is pronounced the General’s the coat is opened, unbuttoned, brushed, re-buttoned and finally left as he hurries away, the garment now mirroring the servile reversal and the collapse of composure.

In lexical terms the garment is named four times in the received English versions, three of those mentions being the proper descriptive, symbolic uses, the fourth being the initial presentation that criticism routinely collapses with the first shift. The three substantive descriptive beats – proud entry → anxious protection → servile retreat – are the three symbolic descriptions of the coat that the critical tradition quotes as the chameleon motif; the four lexical reprises are the entry + the three shifts.

12.text:
In the received Russian text and in the Constance Garnett / usual English versions the шинель / frock-coat / overcoat of Ochumelov / Otchumyelov is not a piece of stage furniture, it is the running index of his chameleon-like self-interest and it is brought back into the narrative at every colour change.

In lexical terms the garment is named four times:

* the entrance presentation – Ochumelov comes up to the crowd “in a frock-coat and a top hat, looking important and impressive” / “in a short grey overcoat with a big white muffler round his neck”, the outward sign of his petty-official self-importance;
* the first fear / deference handling – he pulls / buttons the coat up to his throat, keeps his hands in the pockets of the шинель, is afraid the dog will soil / touch “my overcoat”, the coat becoming the measure of personal risk and contamination;
* the second reversal – when the dog is pronounced a stray he unbuttons / lets the coat fall open, assumes a familiar jocular tone and asserts himself;
* the final reversal / exit – on the news that the dog is almost certainly General Zhigalov’s he again hastily wraps / re-buttons himself in the coat and hurries away servilely.

That is the four explicit coat / шинель notices that the text gives. Literary criticism normally collapses the entrance presentation and the final exit into the three substantive symbolic shifts that the coat registers – proud / official → anxious to keep the coat clean and respectable → servile / chameleonic retreat, i.e. open → closed → open → closed, three distinct changes of mood that the coat is made to embody. In strict lexical count four mentions, three distinct descriptive / symbolic re-descriptions of the coat as the barometer of Ochumelov’s fearful mentality.

6.text:
In the received 1884 text the шинель / frock-coat / overcoat is not stage furniture, it is the external index of Ochumelov/Otchumyelov’s chameleon colour change.

That index is pointed, not merely nominal. In the canonical Russian text and in Garnett / Hingley the garment is brought back into the narrative at the moments when his social colour changes:

* Entrance / establishment. Ochumelov is presented to the crowd already in his official dress, a new / faded overcoat / frock-coat with the muffler, the outward sign of petty-official self-importance and the desire to be seen as in control. It is the baseline against which the vacillation is read;
* First fear-shift. As soon as the dog’s ownership is uncertain he begins to protect himself with the coat – he keeps his hands in the pockets, pulls the coat up to his throat, worries about being soiled / contaminated. The coat is now the measure of his personal risk and anxious self-consciousness;
* Second fear-shift / exit. When the dog is pronounced the General’s dog and then a stray again the coat is handled once more and finally he wraps himself in it and hurries away. The garment is left as the register of deference, humiliation and the completed reversal.

Those are three pointed symbolic descriptive beats, proud → anxious / servile → vacillating / humiliated, which the criticism normally quotes as the coat = mentality motif. In strict lexical terms the garment is named four times in the usual English editions – the entrance presentation plus the three re-handlings – and five times in word-count in Russian, the four/re-presentations mapping onto the three distinct symbolic uses. If the initial presentation is counted as a descriptive shift the text gives four explicit coat notices; the number of substantive symbolic descriptions that track the shifts of his fearful mentality is three.

15.text:
In the received Russian text and in Garnett/Hingley/standard English the шинель/overcoat/frock-coat is not a background prop but the narrative barometer of Ochumelov. It is brought back into the scene at every change of colour in his opinion of the dog.

Lexically the garment is named four times in the canonical versions:

* the entrance presentation: Ochumelov comes on the scene in his new / short grey / official frock-overcoat, the outward sign of petty official self-importance;
* the first handling / fear-of-soiling moment: he pulls the coat up to his throat / keeps his hands in it because the dog might soil him;
* the re-buttoning / self-assertion moment: he loosens / unbuttons / brushes it when the dog is pronounced a stray and he can afford to be harsh/jocular;
* the final wrapping-up / hurried exit: he buttons himself in again and wraps himself in it as he becomes servile again on the news that the dog may be General Zhigalov’s.

Those four verbal reprises are normally collapsed in criticism into three substantive symbolic re-descriptions of the coat, i.e. the initial presentation plus three shifts 
proud/official → anxious/deferential → aggressive/jocular → servile/deferential. 
If the entrance is taken as baseline presentation, the three distinct descriptive beats that carry the chameleon symbolism of Ochumelov’s fearful mentality are the three re-descriptions after the entrance, conventionally rendered as the four-mention / three-shift pattern quoted in the scholarship.

