4.text:
In the Russian original and in the Garnett/Hingley English tradition the шинель/ overcoat / frock-coat is not a piece of stage furniture, it is Ochumelov/Otchumyelov’s barometer. The garment is brought back into the narrative every time his social colour changes.

That gives the classic three-change / four-mention pattern that the criticism always quotes:

* the entrance presentation – Ochumelov comes on the square “in a new / short grey overcoat with a big white muffler round his neck”, i.e. the official self-important pose with which he first takes charge;
* first re-description / first fear-shift – the overcoat is recalled as he is anxious not to be soiled / contaminated by the dog and by the crowd, pulling it round him, keeping his hands in its pockets;
* second re-description / second fear-shift – the overcoat is loosened/unbuttoned in the moment of self-assertion when the dog is pronounced a stray and he can afford to be harsh and familiar;
* final re-description / final reversal – the overcoat is wrapped up / re-buttoned hastily and he hurries away in servile deference when the dog is identified as General Zhigalov’s.

That is four explicit lexical mentions of the coat in the received text, one initial presentation plus three descriptive re-presentations. The three re-presentations are the three substantive symbolic descriptions of the coat that map onto the three chameleon-like changes of his fearful, status-driven mentality – proud → anxious to keep clean → servile. If the initial entrance presentation is counted as a descriptive moment in its own right the garment is named four times; if only the re-descriptions that mark a shift are counted, the symbolic descriptive work is three distinct beats.

