3.text:
The coat / шинель is a running barometer, not a piece of stage furniture, and Chekhov brings it back every time Ochumelov changes colour.

In the received Russian and in Garnett/Hingley the garment is named four times in a strict lexical count:

* entrance presentation – he comes on in his official frock-coat / short grey overcoat with the big white muffler, the outward sign of petty-official self-importance;
* first handling / protection – when the dog may be General Zhigalov’s he pulls/buttons the coat up to his throat, keeps his hands in its pockets and fidgets with it, fear of soiling/contamination;
* second handling / display – when the dog is pronounced a stray he unbuttons/lets the coat fall open and can afford a harsh/jocular tone;
* final wrapping / hurried exit – on the last reversal he hastily re-buttons/wraps himself in the coat again and retreats deferentially.

That is the classic four-mention / three-shift pattern open → closed → open → closed.

The descriptive, symbolic work that is actually tied to the fearful mentality and its shifts is three pointed beats. The entrance is a costume tag / baseline presentation of the official, not yet a descriptive use of the coat to register a change of mood. The symbolic description starts with the first real handling:

1. Anxious protection/fidgeting – the coat as the measure of personal risk when the dog’s ownership is politically dangerous;
2. Self-assertion/loosening – the coat opened/unbuttoned when he can afford to be harsh to Khryukin;
3. Servile retreat/re-wrapping – the coat hastily closed/wrapped on exit when deference returns.

Those three substantive re-descriptions are the three symbolic shifts proud → anxious → servile that the criticism quotes as the coat = mentality motif. The four nominal reprises are the way those three shifts are materially realised in the text, entrance + three re-handlings. If the entrance is counted as a descriptive moment in its own right the text gives four lexical mentions, three of them being the distinct symbolic descriptions of the chameleon-like fear.

7.text:
